                        <tr><td>Voicemeeter strips, buses, settings files and Macro Buttons</td><td data-label="Where">Studio, through Voicemeeter's Remote API on your PC</td></tr>
                        <tr><td>Stream Deck keys</td><td data-label="Where">The CertiFried plugin in the Stream Deck app, talking to Studio on 127.0.0.1</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                </table></div>
                <p>Studio holds no automations of its own. It asks the server for your automation list (for hotkeys and buttons) and your voice phrases, and it tells the server what your OBS looks like so the editor can suggest scene and source names.</p>

                <h2 id="pairing">Pairing</h2>
                <p>Studio has to prove it belongs to your account once. There are two ways:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Sign in with CertiFried.</strong> Studio opens your browser on certifriedmultitool.com. If the page shows the same four-character code as Studio, choose Approve. The request works once and only for 10 minutes.</li>
                    <li><strong>Pairing code.</strong> On the dashboard's Devices page, make an eight-character code and type it into Studio. A code works once, for 10 minutes.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>Either way Studio receives a device token. Studio keeps it encrypted on your PC; the server keeps only a hash of it, so the token cannot be read back from our database. The device shows on the Devices page under the name Studio gives it (<code><%= _f.deviceName %></code> unless you choose another). <strong>Revoke</strong> there stops it at once. <strong>Unpair this PC</strong> in Studio deletes the token from your PC; revoke it on the dashboard as well.</p>

                <h2 id="vod-safe-music">VOD-safe music</h2>
                <p>Song requests must not end up in your saved Twitch VODs. With OBS's Advanced output, Twitch receives the live mix plus a separate VOD track that becomes the VOD's audio. While a song request command is switched on in your automations, Studio keeps the music off that track:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li>Music plays only through one OBS browser source, <strong>CertiFried Music</strong>, set to be controlled by OBS (so it never goes through Windows desktop audio) and monitored so you hear it.</li>
                    <li>Its audio goes on every track except the VOD track. Studio checks this when OBS connects, when scenes or profiles change, every minute, and right before every song.</li>
                    <li>If the VOD track is off, Studio turns it on while you are neither streaming nor recording, after copying your OBS profile file.</li>
                    <li>If you are live and protection is not active, music stays paused. Chat is told requests are paused.</li>
                </ul>
                <p>When no song request command is on, Studio changes nothing in OBS for music: no source, no VOD track, no tracks. It still lists audio sources on your VOD track that look like music, and warns about Desktop Audio, for your information. If you switch song requests off after the CertiFried Music source was made, the source stays where it is (Studio stops looking after it) and Studio offers a Remove the CertiFried Music source button; it never removes it by itself. Switch song requests on and protection starts at once.</p>
                <p>This is best effort, and some things it cannot cover. <strong>Twitch clips</strong> are cut from the live stream, so they contain the music. <strong>Kick</strong> has no VOD track, so music Kick receives live is in Kick's VODs (with the obs-multi-rtmp plugin you can give the Kick output its own audio track without music). <strong>Local recordings</strong> include the music unless you turn on Record without music in Studio. Other audio on the VOD track, such as Desktop Audio or another sound alert tool, is reported in Studio but never changed unless you click <strong>Keep out of VOD</strong>.</p>

                <h2 id="voice-commands">Voice commands</h2>
                <p>Add a Voice command trigger to an automation and list its phrases. In Studio, turn the microphone on (it is off by default) and choose always listening or push-to-talk. Studio passes the audio to the speech recogniser built into Windows, on your PC, which listens only for your phrases. Nothing is recorded or saved, and no audio leaves your PC; when a phrase is recognised, only the phrase text and how confident the match was are sent to CertiFried to start the automation.</p>

                <h2 id="docks-and-theme">Docks, theme and setup</h2>
                <p>Studio's <strong>Set up OBS</strong> checklist offers three changes. Each says what it will do and runs only when you click it.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Docks</strong>: Activity, Controls and Chat panels inside OBS. Studio writes them into OBS's own settings while OBS is closed (OBS rewrites that file when it exits), after a backup, and keeps your other docks. Each dock address carries a dock key that can only show your feed and run your automations; replace it from Studio or revoke it on Devices.</li>
                    <li><strong>Theme</strong>: Studio copies the CertiFried, CertiFried Light and CertiFried Sand themes into OBS's themes folder. You choose one in OBS under Settings, Appearance.</li>
                    <li><strong>Starter scenes</strong>: Starting Soon, Be Right Back and Ending, only if you do not have them.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="overlays">Overlays</h2>
                <p>Alerts, goals, event lists, counters, now playing and chat overlays are OBS browser sources on certifriedmultitool.com, each with its own key. Studio can add them to the scene on air for you (Set up OBS). Nothing about viewers is stored by them: alerts and chat are sent live.</p>

                <h2 id="lights">Lights</h2>
                <p>In Studio, open Lights and choose Find lights. Only then does Studio search your network: Govee's LAN discovery (switch on LAN control for each light in the Govee Home app), mDNS for Philips Hue bridges, Nanoleaf panels and Elgato Key Lights and Light Strips, and LIFX's LAN broadcast; Logitech Litra Glow, Beam and Beam LX are found over USB. After that it looks again each time it starts. A Hue bridge is paired by pressing its button, a Nanoleaf by holding its power button; the token stays on your PC. Studio talks to a Hue bridge over HTTPS and remembers the bridge's certificate the first time, refusing a bridge that later shows a different one. It uses the bridge's normal API, not the Entertainment streaming mode.</p>
                <p>Automations can set a colour or white, change brightness, turn lights on or off, start a scene or effect the light offers, flash or pulse and then put the light back as it was, take a colour from a variable (such as a viewer's <code>!color red</code>), and apply a light preset you made on the Devices page. Govee and LIFX lights can also work through their makers' cloud with your own key, added on the Devices page; the CertiFried server uses it within the makers' rate limits, without Studio.</p>

                <h2 id="voicemeeter">Voicemeeter</h2>
                <p>Off until you turn it on under Audio. Studio then uses Voicemeeter's own Remote API (VoicemeeterRemote64.dll from your Voicemeeter install) to mute and unmute strips and buses, set or change their gain with an optional fade, load a settings file you saved in Documents\Voicemeeter, and press Macro Buttons. It never starts Voicemeeter and loads only files from that folder.</p>

                <h2 id="stream-deck">Stream Deck</h2>
                <p>Turn on Stream Deck in Studio's Settings, then install the CertiFried plugin from there (or from the download page). Its keys run an automation, switch scene, show or hide a source, mute an input, change a counter, play a sound or apply a light preset, and show the scene on air, mute state, source state and counter value. The plugin talks only to Studio on the same PC (127.0.0.1), with a token Studio shows you; Studio refuses connections from web pages.</p>

                <h2 id="changes-to-obs">What Studio changes in OBS</h2>
                <p>Without asking, only what VOD-safe music needs, and only while a song request command is switched on: Studio adds the CertiFried Music browser source to your scenes, sets its audio tracks, and turns on the Twitch VOD track in your OBS profile while you are offline (after copying the profile file to <code>basic.ini.certifried-backup-&lt;time&gt;</code>). Streamers who do not use song requests get no change at all. Everything else, docks, the theme, starter scenes, overlays, the WebSocket settings, recording tracks, removing the music source and your other sources, changes only when you click.</p>

                <h2 id="offline">When something is offline</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>Studio closed or disconnected</strong>: OBS, sound and hotkey actions wait two minutes (or the time you set on the action), then expire and show as expired in Run history. Studio reconnects by itself, waiting a little longer after each failed try.</li>
                    <li><strong>CertiFried unreachable</strong>: Studio keeps trying. Its local hotkeys (play a sound, stop sounds, OBS actions) still work; hotkeys that run an automation need the server.</li>
                    <li><strong>OBS closed</strong>: OBS actions fail and are reported as failed. Song requests pause until OBS is back and the VOD track is protected again.</li>
                    <li><strong>Two copies with the same pairing</strong>: the newer one takes over and the other stops, so they never fight.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2 id="updates">Updates and checksums</h2>
                <p>The installed app checks <code>certifriedmultitool.com/downloads/studio/</code> shortly after it starts and then every <%= _f.updateHours %> hours. A new version downloads in the background and is checked against the SHA-512 hash in the update feed before it is installed, when you quit Studio or choose Restart to update. The portable app cannot replace itself; it tells you when a newer version is on the download page. You can turn update checks off in Studio's Settings.</p>
                <p>The download page shows the SHA-256 checksum of each build and links <code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code>. To check a file in PowerShell: <code>Get-FileHash .\CertiFried-Studio-Setup-x.y.z.exe</code>, then compare the result with the page.</p>

                <h2 id="smartscreen">The SmartScreen warning</h2>
                <p>Studio is not code-signed and will stay that way: a signing certificate costs money every year, and CertiFried charges nobody. The first time you open it, Windows SmartScreen shows "Windows protected your PC". Click <strong>More info</strong>, then <strong>Run anyway</strong>. If you want to be sure the file is ours, compare its checksum first, or read and build the <a href="<%= _f.githubRepo %>" rel="noopener">source code</a> yourself.</p>
